'''veck†''' (vek), ''n.'' [ME. ''vecke, vekke;'' origin obscure.] An old woman.
{{smaller block|A rympled ''vekke,'' ferre ronne in age.
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose,'' l. 4495.}}}}

'''vection†''' (vek′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''vectio(n-),'' a carrying, conveyance, < ''vehere,'' pp. ''vectus,'' bear, convey: see ''vehicle.''] The act of carrying, or the state of being carried; vectitation; “a carrying or portage,” ''Blount'' (1670).

'''vectis''' (vek′tis), ''n.'' [L., a pole, bar, bolt, spike.] '''1.''' In ''Rom. antiq.,'' a bolt.—'''2.''' [NL.] In ''obstet.,'' a curved fenestrated instrument similar to one of the blades of the obstetrical forceps, used in certain cases to aid delivery. Commonly called ''lever.''

'''vectitation''' (vek-ti-tā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. *''vectitare,'' pp. ''vectitatus,'' bear or carry about, freq. of ''vehere,'' pp. ''vectus,'' convey: see ''vection.''] A carrying, or the state of being carried. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Their enervated lords are lolling in their chariots (a species of ''vectitation'' seldom used amongst the ancients except by old men).
{{right|''Martinus Scriblerus.''}}}}

'''vector''' (vek′to̤r), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [= F. ''vecteur,'' < L. ''vector,'' one who carries or conveys, < ''vehere,'' pp. ''vectus,'' carry, convey: see ''vection.''] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' (''a'') In quaternions, a quantity which, being added to any point of space, gives as the sum that point which is at a certain distance in a certain direction from the first. {{smaller|Vectors are said to be equal when their directions and magnitudes are the same. Unit}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller|vectors in quaternions are considered as equivalent to quadrantal versors having their axes in the directions of vectors; the word ''vector'' has accordingly sometimes, but incorrectly, been used in the sense of a quadrantal versor. Every quaternion can be resolved in one way, and one way only, into a sum of a scalar and a vector; and this vector is called the ''vector of the quaternion'', and is denoted by writing V before the sign of the quaternion. Thus, Vq denotes the vector of the quaternion q.}} Hence — (''b'') A directive quantity; a quantity determined by two numbers giving its direction and a third giving its magnitude.—'''2.''' Same as ''radius vector''. See ''radius''.—{{smaller|'''Addition of vectors.''' See ''addition''.—'''Origin of a vector.''' See ''origin''.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Of the nature of or concerned with vectors.—{{smaller|'''Vector analysis,''' the algebra of vectors.—'''Vector equation,''' an equation between vectors.—'''Vector function.''' See ''function''.—'''Vector potential,''' a vector quantity so distributed throughout space that the result of operating upon it by the Hamiltonian operator represents some natural quantity.}}

'''vectorial''' (vek-tō′ri-ạl), ''a.'' [< ''vector'' + ''-ial''.] Of or pertaining to a vector or vectors.—{{smaller|'''Vectorial coördinates.''' See ''coördinate''.}}

'''vecture†''' (vek′tūr), ''n.'' [= F. ''voiture'' = It. ''vettura'', a carriage, < L. ''vectura'', a carrying, transportation, < ''vehere'', pp. ''vectus'', carry: see ''vection''.] A carrying; carriage; conveyance by carrying. ''Bacon'', Seditions and Troubles (ed. 1887).

